 "equations": [],
 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Function",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "Each line pin has its own back-to-back clamp to pin 3, so a line may carry signals that swing both above and below ground. Each line is rated for 130 W of surge with an 8/20 µs pulse."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Surge and ESD ratings",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "Surge figures are for one 8/20 µs exponentially decaying pulse per IEC 61000-4-5, measured from pin 1 or pin 2 to pin 3. The 30 kV contact rating holds after ten IEC 61000-4-2 discharges. Peak pulse power falls linearly with junction temperature from its full value at 25 °C to zero at 150 °C."
  },
  {
   "topic": "IEC 61000-4-2 test pulse",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "The IEC 61000-4-2 ESD current pulse rises from 10 % to 90 % in tr = 0.6 ns to 1 ns to its 100 % peak, falls to a dip, recovers to a second hump, and decays; reference instants are marked at 30 ns and 60 ns."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Clamping at 1 kV",
   "page": 6,
   "text": "With a ±1 kV IEC 61000-4-2 discharge, the open-circuit pulse peaks near ±1 kV and decays over about 300 ns. With the diode in place, the line shows a spike of about +28 V (positive discharge) or -35 V (negative discharge), then sits near ±5 V for roughly 100 ns before returning to 0 V."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Board layout",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "ESD, EFT and surge suppression depends heavily on layout. Mount the diode as near the input connector as the board allows, with a short trace to the line it guards. Run as few parallel signal paths as possible and keep guarded and unguarded traces apart. Keep every conductive loop small, supply and ground loops among them. Return the transient to ground by a short path of its own rather than one shared at a common point, using ground planes and, on multilayer boards, ground vias."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Qualification",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "Qualified to AEC-Q101 for discrete semiconductors and suited to automotive use."
  }
 ],
